    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <h2 id="how-paye-works" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">1. How PAYE & Salary Tax Works in NZ</h2>
    <p>
      In New Zealand, the tax system for salary and wage earners is remarkably streamlined compared to many other countries, thanks to the <strong>Pay As You Earn (PAYE)</strong> system. Your employer automatically calculates and withholds the correct amount of income tax from your salary every pay cycle—whether you are paid weekly, fortnightly, or monthly. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Your tax liability is determined by progressive tax brackets. This means you do not pay a flat rate on all your income. Instead, your income is divided into slices, and each slice is taxed at a specific rate. This is known as your <em>marginal tax rate</em>. It is a common misconception that moving into a higher tax bracket reduces your overall take-home pay. In reality, you only pay the higher rate on the earnings that fall within that specific top bracket, meaning earning a raise always results in more money in your pocket.
    </p>

    <!-- Tax Brackets Table -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="min-w-full border border-hairline text-left text-sm dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft font-semibold text-ink dark:bg-surface dark:text-on-primary">
          <tr>
            <th class="border p-3">Income Bracket (NZD)</th>
            <th class="border p-3">PAYE Tax Rate</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Notes</th>
          </tr>
