          <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">$70,001 – $180,000</td><td class="p-3 font-semibold text-primary">33.0%</td><td class="p-3">Higher earnings band</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">Above $180,000</td><td class="p-3 font-semibold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">39.0%</td><td class="p-3">Top marginal tax tier</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <h2 id="paye-statutory-deductions" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">2. Statutory Deductions (ACC Levy, KiwiSaver, Student Loan)</h2>
    <p>
      In addition to PAYE income tax, standard salary paychecks in New Zealand include several statutory deductions. It is critical to understand these because they significantly bridge the gap between your gross salary and your net take-home pay. 
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-6 text-sm">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-1">ACC Earners' Levy</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted mb-2">Mandatory 1.60% levy for non-work injury compensation cover (capped at $142,283 max earnings). This is flat across all wage earners regardless of tax code.</p>
        <span class="inline-block rounded bg-canvas-soft px-2 py-1 text-xs font-mono">1.60%</span>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-1">KiwiSaver</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted mb-2">Employee retirement saving deduction. You can choose to contribute 3%, 4%, 6%, 8%, or 10% of your gross pay. Employers are mandated to match a minimum of 3%.</p>
        <span class="inline-block rounded bg-canvas-soft px-2 py-1 text-xs font-mono">3% – 10%</span>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-1">Student Loan</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted mb-2">If you have a New Zealand student loan, deductions are made at 12% on every dollar earned above the annual repayment threshold ($24,128/year).</p>
        <span class="inline-block rounded bg-canvas-soft px-2 py-1 text-xs font-mono">12% over limit</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Tip on KiwiSaver:</strong> Choosing the right KiwiSaver rate is a balancing act. While a 3% contribution maximizes your immediate take-home pay, opting for 4%, 6%, 8%, or 10% leverages compound interest for a more secure retirement. Furthermore, if you are saving for your first home, maximizing your KiwiSaver contributions early on can significantly boost your deposit through the First Home Grant and savings withdrawals.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 3 -->
    <h2 id="salary-example-90k" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">3. Detailed Walkthrough: Tax on $90,000 Salary</h2>
    <p>
      Theory is great, but applying it to real numbers brings clarity. Let's examine a complete breakdown of <strong>tax on a $90,000 salary in NZ</strong>. In this example, we assume the individual has opted for the standard 3% KiwiSaver contribution and has already paid off their student loan (so no 12% deduction applies).
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="min-w-full border border-hairline text-left text-sm dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft font-semibold text-ink dark:bg-surface dark:text-on-primary">
          <tr>
            <th class="border p-3">Calculation Item</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Annual Amount (NZD)</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Monthly Amount</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Weekly Amount</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline dark:divide-overlay-lighter">
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-bold">Gross Salary</td><td class="p-3 font-bold">$90,000</td><td class="p-3">$7,500</td><td class="p-3">$1,731</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">PAYE Income Tax</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">-$19,630</td><td class="p-3">-$1,636</td><td class="p-3">-$377</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">ACC Earners Levy (1.60%)</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">-$1,440</td><td class="p-3">-$120</td><td class="p-3">-$28</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">KiwiSaver Employee (3%)</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">-$2,700</td><td class="p-3">-$225</td><td class="p-3">-$52</td></tr>
          <tr class="bg-green-500/10 font-bold">
            <td class="p-3 text-green-700 dark:text-green-300">Net Take-Home Pay</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-green-700 dark:text-green-300">$66,230</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-green-700 dark:text-green-300">$5,519</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-green-700 dark:text-green-300">$1,273</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      As you can see, on a $90,000 gross salary, the effective tax rate (including ACC) is around 23.4%. By understanding these exact figures, employees can make highly informed decisions regarding their personal budgets, housing affordability, and discretionary spending.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 4 -->
    <h2 id="tax-writeoffs-non-taxable" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">4. Tax Write-Offs & Non-Taxable Allowances</h2>
    <p>
      One of the best ways to legally enhance your take-home pay in New Zealand is through employer allowances. While standard salaried employees cannot generally claim personal tax deductions for work-related expenses directly on their tax returns like independent contractors can, employers can provide tax-free reimbursements and allowances under IRD guidelines:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-4">
      <li><strong>Work From Home (WFH) Allowance:</strong> Post-pandemic, the IRD introduced simplified rules for WFH expenses. Employers can pay an allowance of up to $5 per week for telecommunications and $15 to $20 per week for home utilities/furniture entirely tax-free, without requiring receipts. This is a mutually beneficial arrangement that increases your net income without raising the employer's PAYE obligations.</li>
      <li><strong>Mileage Reimbursements:</strong> If you use your personal vehicle for business purposes (excluding your regular commute from home to work), your employer can reimburse you using the IRD's standard mileage rates. This reimbursement is not subject to tax.</li>
      <li><strong>Uniform, Tools, and Safety Gear:</strong> If your job requires specific safety gear, a distinctive uniform, or specialized tools, any employer reimbursement for the purchase or laundering of these items is typically exempt from PAYE tax.</li>
      <li><strong>Relocation Expenses:</strong> If you are required to relocate for work (e.g., moving from Auckland to Christchurch), eligible relocation expenses paid for by your employer are usually considered non-taxable, providing a massive financial relief during a stressful transition.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Section 5 -->
    <h2 id="tax-credits-ietc" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">5. Independent Earner Credit & Low Income Relief</h2>
    <p>
      New Zealand offers several targeted tax credits designed to ease the financial burden on lower and middle-income earners, effectively increasing their take-home pay:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-4">
      <li><strong>Independent Earner Tax Credit (IETC):</strong> The IETC provides a direct tax credit of <strong>$10 per week ($520 per year)</strong> for individuals earning between $24,000 and $48,000 annually. To qualify, you must not be receiving an income-tested benefit, Working for Families Tax Credits, or New Zealand Superannuation. This credit is often applied directly to your tax code (e.g., "ME" tax code), meaning you receive the benefit incrementally in every paycheck.</li>
      <li><strong>Working for Families Tax Credits:</strong> Aimed at low-to-middle income families with dependent children, these credits can significantly augment household income. The amount depends on your combined family income, the number of children you have, and their ages.</li>
      <li><strong>Best Start Tax Credit:</strong> Available to all families in the first year of a child's life, providing a weekly payment regardless of household income.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Section 6 -->
    <h2 id="residual-income-tax" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">6. Residual Income Tax (RIT) & myIR Online Portal</h2>
    <p>
      At the end of the financial year (which runs from 1 April to 31 March), the IRD conducts a final reconciliation of the tax you have paid versus the tax you actually owe. The final amount of tax liability, after deducting all PAYE withheld and any tax credits, is known as your <strong>Residual Income Tax (RIT)</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your PAYE deductions were completely accurate and you had no other income sources, your RIT should be close to zero. However, if you have additional untaxed income—such as interest from bank accounts, dividends, rental income, or a side hustle—you might end up with a tax bill. Conversely, if you were over-taxed (perhaps you worked part of the year or your income fluctuated heavily), you may receive a tax refund. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The easiest way to manage your taxes, view your end-of-year tax summary, check your real-time PAYE deductions, and securely pay any tax owed is by registering for a <strong>myIR</strong> account via the Inland Revenue website (<code>ird.govt.nz</code>). The platform provides complete transparency, allowing you to track exactly where your hard-earned money is going.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 7 -->
    <h2 id="secondary-tax" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">7. Managing Secondary Tax Codes for Side Hustles</h2>
    <p>
      In today's economy, many New Zealanders maintain a side hustle or a second part-time job. It is a persistent myth that secondary income is penalized with a "higher" tax rate. In truth, New Zealand taxes total global income. 
    </p>
    <p>
      When you have a second job, you must use a secondary tax code (such as SB, S, SH, or ST) based on your estimated total annual income from all sources. This ensures the correct marginal rate is applied to your secondary earnings, preventing you from receiving a massive, unexpected tax bill at the end of the financial year. If you accidentally overpay during the year because of an incorrect secondary code, the IRD's automatic end-of-year assessment process will calculate your RIT and issue a refund.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 8 FAQs -->
