    <div class="space-y-4 mt-6">
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How do I calculate my take-home pay in New Zealand?</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm">Your take-home pay (net salary) is calculated by taking your gross annual salary and deducting PAYE income tax, the mandatory ACC Earners' Levy (1.60%), your KiwiSaver employee contribution (3%, 4%, 6%, 8%, or 10%), and any student loan repayments (12% of earnings above the repayment threshold).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How much tax do I pay on a $90,000 salary in New Zealand?</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm">On a gross salary of $90,000 NZD, income tax is approximately $19,630, plus an ACC earners levy of $1,440. If contributing 3% to KiwiSaver ($2,700), your annual net take-home pay is approximately $66,230 (or roughly $1,273 per week).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the ACC Earners' Levy in NZ?</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm">A statutory 1.60% deduction from employment wages to cover non-work accident injury insurance. It is mandatory for all wage earners and is capped at a maximum earnings limit of $142,283 per year.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is non-taxable income in New Zealand?</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm">Non-taxable income includes tax-exempt employer reimbursements for genuine work expenses (like specific home office costs), certain relocation allowances, gift inheritances, and capital gains on primary residence sales.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What does Residual Income Tax (RIT) mean in NZ?</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm">Residual Income Tax (RIT) is the final tax balance owed to IRD after subtracting all tax withheld and tax credits. If you owe more than $5,000 in RIT, you will generally be required to pay provisional tax in the following year.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

